  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nThe potent sub-tropical ridge per water vapor imagery continues to \ndominate the southeast CONUS, pushing west and north into the \ncentral plains. Boundary layer mixing overnight continues to inhibit \nthe strong radiational cooling as morning lows are generally mild in \nthe low 70s. During the daytime, strong warm and moisture advection \naid gusty south winds from 10 to 15 mph, increasing to over 20 mph \nsustained by Tuesday. Given the synoptic setup, confidence remains \nhigh that most of the CWA will see peak heat indices from 100 to 105 \ndegrees this holiday weekend through as late as Wednesday. The \nExtreme Heat Warning remains in place with no changes made through \nTuesday evening. Glimpses of heat relief are seen on the northern \nperiphery of the ridge by Sunday afternoon as a back door cold front \nslides south into northern MO, impacting portions of far northeast \nand eastern Kansas in the form of cloud cover, somewhat cooler temps \nand/or a few showers/storms (less than 20%). Otherwise strong \nsubsidence and capping in the mid levels should suppress organized \nconvection in our area through at least Tuesday. \n\nLatest ensemble guidance begins to diverge further regarding the \ntrack of a upper shortwave trough skirting the northern plains and \nGreat Lakes by the middle of next week. In turn, this would shift a \ncold front southward towards north central Kansas, returning the \nchances for showers and storms and perhaps slightly cooler temps. \nSeveral ensemble members however hold the boundary further north, \ntranslated by overall NBM probabilities of QPF greater than 0.01 \ninches being around 20% or less. For the time being, will maintain \nthe low chance pops through the middle of next week and a less \ncertain temp forecast in the low 90s for highs. There persists a 10 \ndegree spread from 25th-75th percentiles for high temps Wed.-Fri. \nand will depend on placement of the front and subsequent cloud cover.",
